EVERY IF-THEN AND HINT HALFWORD.

This document accounts for EVERY sixteen-bit Thumb halfword from
hexadecimal 0xBF00 to hexadecimal 0xBFFF, inclusive, and no others.
It does not assert that all possible complete instruction streams
containing these halfwords are legal or executable.

Status: experimental English Major C ARMv7-A T32 instruction-encoding
subtree, cross-checked against ARM DDI 0406C.d. NOT full-ISA acceptance,
a working compiler, a complete CPU interpreter, or a hardware execution.

NORMATIVE SOURCES.

Arm Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R edition,
ARM DDI 0406C.d, publication ID040418:
  A6.2.5, Table A6-7: sixteen-bit If-Then and hint instructions;
  A8.8.55, IT T1: firstcond and mask, conditional ITSTATE;
  A8.8.18, B T1: conditional B is NOT permitted inside IT;
  the architecture's exception and conditional-execution rules.
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e

This is NOT the ARMv7-M manual and NOT a modern Armv8-A extension
rule silently applied backwards.

The complete data-only enumeration of all 256 encodings is:
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-if-then-and-hint-halfwords.tsv
at repository root. It records the high and low nibble,
the decoded architecture class, number of IT instruction
SLOTS, and whether the current English Major C grant allows
this form in its specified context.

OPERATIONS THAT WERE ALREADY GRANTED.

Operation 46 in the sibling closed grant permits the legal
T32 IT instruction, subject to exact encoding, predicates,
length, following-instruction restrictions and surrounding
ITSTATE.

Operation 52 permits the architectural NOP instruction.
It does not grant all encodings that happen to perform
NOP-like work on one particular processor.

YIELD, WFE, WFI and SEV are architectures' named hint
operations but have not been granted long executable-English
operation names in the current source vocabulary.
Their decoding in this table is not a grant to use them.

PART ONE. EXACT BIT PARTITION OF THE 256 HALFWORDS.

In this subtree the upper eight bits are binary 10111111,
which is hexadecimal BF.

The next four bits, numbered 7..4, are called firstcond
when the instruction decodes as IT and called opA in the
original miscellaneous-instruction decode table.

The lowest four bits, numbered 3..0, are called mask
when the instruction decodes as IT and called opB in the
original miscellaneous-instruction decode table.

The 16 possible firstcond/opA values multiplied by
the 16 possible mask/opB values yield 256 distinct
halfwords and no overlaps.

There are exactly TWO top-level cases:

CASE ONE: the lowest four bits are all zero.
The instruction occupies TWO bytes and belongs to the
hint decoding space; it is NOT an IT instruction.

CASE TWO: at least one of the lowest four bits is one.
The instruction occupies TWO bytes and is assigned
to IT's T1 encoding family, whose firstcond must then
be examined for additional legality restrictions.

PART TWO. THE SIXTEEN HINT-SPACE VALUES.

When mask equals zero, the sixteen possible opA/firstcond
values produce sixteen distinct encodings, one for each
BF00, BF10, BF20, ..., BFF0.

BF00 has class NOP T1, the no-operation hint.
The present long-name operation 52 admits BF00,
subject to the ordinary processor state and memory
access premise. This does not imply that NOP
takes no time, uses no energy, or causes no
microarchitectural fetch activity.

BF10 has class YIELD T1.
BF20 has class WFE T1, wait for event.
BF30 has class WFI T1, wait for interrupt.
BF40 has class SEV T1, send event.

Each is a distinct architecturally named hint and
may affect scheduling, waiting, event signalling,
or execution environment behavior according to
the exact processor and platform. These are NOT
ordinary 32-bit arithmetic operations and cannot
be turned into granted NOPs merely for convenience.
All four are decoded but UNGRANTED in the current
English Major C initial operation list.

The eleven remaining encodings are:
    BF50, BF60, BF70, BF80, BF90,
    BFA0, BFB0, BFC0, BFD0, BFE0, BFF0.

ARM Table A6-7 classifies these as UNALLOCATED HINTS:
the architecture says such encodings execute as NOPs,
but software MUST NOT use them. Therefore the present
source profile explicitly REJECTS their use, even
though a physical processor may have a benign
NOP-compatible behavior today.

These eleven forms are NOT the same thing as the
permanently undefined UDF halfwords DE00..DEFF
and are NOT Supervisor Call encodings DF00..DFFF.
The source must keep those distinctions, rather
than saying that every rejected operation traps.

The diagnostic LLVM 17 Thumb disassembler displays
the unallocated forms with a generic HINT mnemonic.
For example BF50 appears as "hint #0x5".
The existence of such a printed mnemonic does not
overrule the software-must-not-use restriction.

PART THREE. WHAT AN IT HALFWORD ACTUALLY DECLARES.

When mask is nonzero, the halfword is in the
IT T1 decoding family. IT does not branch.
It declares a sequence of ONE THROUGH FOUR
following T32 INSTRUCTIONS whose data effects
are conditional on the encoded firstcond
and a specified Then/Else pattern.

An instruction in an IT block may occupy two
or four bytes; "four instruction slots" never
means "exactly four halfwords" or "exactly
eight bytes". A four-byte instruction counts
as ONE IT slot even though it is fetched
as TWO successive 16-bit halfwords.

The IT instruction itself occupies TWO bytes.
It establishes or updates ITSTATE, which
must be accounted for as architectural
condition-control state. The following
instructions advance ITSTATE according to
the architecture's conditional-execution rules.
Their exact register and memory effects depend
on their own encodings AND whether the relevant
IT condition holds.

IT itself does NOT change N, Z, C or V and does
not execute the following instructions merely
because it has identified them. It is a control
context for how those instructions execute
when their addresses become current.

A later branch to an instruction in the
middle of an IT block, other than a
properly permitted exception return
restoring appropriate status, is not
a valid checked program entry.

PART FOUR. COUNT THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS, NOT THE BYTES.

For mask not equal to zero, count the consecutive
zero bits at its least significant end.

A mask whose least significant one is at bit
three selects ONE following instruction slot.
A mask whose least significant one is at bit
two selects TWO following instruction slots.
A mask whose least significant one is at bit
one selects THREE following instruction slots.
A mask whose least significant one is at bit
zero selects FOUR following instruction slots.

Equivalently, number_of_slots = four minus
number_of_trailing_zero_bits_in_the_four_bit_mask.

Examples:
  BF08: firstcond EQ, mask 1000, ONE slot, IT EQ.
  BF0C: firstcond EQ, mask 1100, TWO slots, ITE EQ.
  BF04: firstcond EQ, mask 0100, TWO slots, ITT EQ.
  BF01: firstcond EQ, mask 0001, FOUR slots.
  BF0F: firstcond EQ, mask 1111, FOUR slots.

The mask's other bits determine Then and Else
conditions according to the precise ITSTATE
state machine. A compiler must not infer
every slot's condition merely from the
NUMBER of slots. The exact sequence must
be decoded from the mask and firstcond.
A full independent state-transition audit
of every ITSTATE intermediate step remains
required before declaring a complete
executable IT interpreter.

There are 15 possible nonzero masks for each
four-bit firstcond, giving 240 IT-family encodings.

PART FIVE. FIRST CONDITION LEGALITY IS NOT OPTIONAL.

ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.55 gives TWO important
UNPREDICTABLE cases for IT T1:

(1) firstcond equals binary 1111, hexadecimal F,
    regardless of which nonzero mask was encoded.

(2) firstcond equals binary 1110, hexadecimal E,
    AND the mask has a number of set bits other
    than exactly ONE.

It also declares that executing IT while an
IT block is already active is UNPREDICTABLE.

For firstcond from hexadecimal zero through D
inclusive, all 15 nonzero mask encodings are
allocated IT T1 forms, for 14*15=210 forms.
These still require a valid surrounding
execution context and following instruction stream.

For firstcond E, only the four masks 0001,0010,
0100,1000 have exactly one bit set; these
four forms can express an IT block with AL
as initial condition under the stated ARMv7-A
rule. Other firstcond-E masks, eleven in count,
are UNPREDICTABLE and REJECTED by English Major C.

For firstcond F, all fifteen nonzero masks
are UNPREDICTABLE and REJECTED.

Thus the 240 IT-family bit patterns partition into:
  214 conditionally admissible IT encodings;
   26 UNPREDICTABLE IT encodings.
Their counts sum to 240.

Combined with the hint encodings:
  214 conditionally permitted IT;
   26 rejected UNPREDICTABLE IT;
    1 permitted NOP;
    4 decoded-but-ungranted named hints;
   11 rejected software-reserved unallocated hints;
  256 total.

The 214 "permitted IT" entries are NOT permissions
to place them inside one another, to ignore ITSTATE,
or to conditionally execute an instruction which is
independently prohibited inside IT. For instance
a 16-bit B T1 from D000..DDFF is NOT permitted
inside an IT block, even as the last instruction.

PART SIX. TWO SIMILAR LOOKING ENCODINGS, DIFFERENT LEGALITY.

Hexadecimal BFE8 has firstcond E (AL) and mask 8,
which has ONE set bit. The ARMv7-A T1 rule
does not reject that form on firstcond grounds.
The foreign LLVM decoder displays "it al".
A checked program must nevertheless account
for ITSTATE and the following instruction.

Hexadecimal BFF8 has firstcond F and mask 8.
It is architecturally UNPREDICTABLE under the
pinned ARM manual, and the initial English Major C
grant REJECTS the encoding.

The foreign LLVM decoder ALSO prints "it al"
for BFF8. That spelling is NOT evidence that
the architecture treats BFF8 as a legal IT AL,
and is not an appropriate source of permission.
The encoded firstcond bits, not the diagnostic
mnemonic's friendly display, decide whether
a candidate is admitted.

Likewise hexadecimal BFE3 has firstcond E and
mask 3 (binary 0011), whose bit population
count is two. It is UNPREDICTABLE. LLVM 17
prints an IT-looking mnemonic but also
annotates an unpredictable predicate sequence.
The architectural legality rule is stronger
than either diagnostic presentation choice.

PART SEVEN. INSTRUCTION-FLOW EXAMPLES WITHOUT TEACHING SYNTAX.

Example A: suppose 0xBF08 is at byte address A.
This is IT EQ with one following instruction slot.
The instruction at A+2 may occupy TWO or FOUR bytes.
If its own encoding is permitted in IT and Z is
one, its permitted effects occur.
If Z is zero, its conditional data-register
effects do not occur, but architectural
instruction sequencing still advances past
its ACTUAL encoded length, updating ITSTATE.
An instruction fetch or decode of its bytes
does not imply its register write executed.

Example B: suppose 0xBF0C begins at byte
address A. It encodes ITE EQ with two
instruction slots: the first is governed
by EQ and the second by the inverted
condition NE. If both following instructions
are two bytes long, they begin at A+2
and A+4. If the first is FOUR bytes long,
the second begins at A+6, not A+4.
The slot count remains two in both cases.

Example C: suppose 0xBF00 is encountered
outside any unusual execution context.
The current grant's operation 52 permits
its NOP behavior, and the execution
index normally advances by TWO bytes.
That permission does not extend to 0xBF50,
even though 0xBF50 may execute as a
NOP-compatible unallocated hint.

Example D: if an authorized IT block
is active and the next encoded machine
instruction is a 16-bit conditional B T1
such as 0xD000, the exact T1 rule
declares this combination UNPREDICTABLE.
The checked language must REJECT the
combination rather than applying the
generic statement "branches are allowed
in the last IT slot".

PART EIGHT. RETAIN THE CONSIDERED DESIGN CHOICES.

Choice A: use actual T32 IT blocks when
the emitted instructions and predicates
are architecturally legal. Benefit:
predication without an additional
control-transfer instruction in some
short sequences. Cost: four-slot limit,
implicit ITSTATE, flag/branch restrictions.

Choice B: replace conditional arithmetic
with explicit short T1 conditional B
outside IT. Benefit: no implicit ITSTATE
for following instructions. Cost: extra
branch and possible branch-prediction cost;
the T1 branch reach is limited.

Choice C: select 32-bit Thumb conditional
branch encodings for longer control
transfers. Benefit: increased reach.
Cost: different bit decoding and IT
constraints, plus instruction width.

Choice D: compute data masks and use
ordinary unconditional arithmetic in
place of predicated operations. Benefit:
may avoid a data-dependent branch.
Cost: extra instructions and semantic
proofs needed for signedness, flags,
overflow, faults, and performance.

Choice E: intentionally issue named WFI,
WFE, SEV or YIELD instructions after
a separately reviewed environment grant.
Benefit: explicit control over waiting,
event signalling, or scheduling hints.
Cost: hardware/OS privilege and liveness
effects that cannot be replaced by NOP.

Choice F: treat every currently
unallocated hint as executable NOP
because the ARM architecture describes
NOP-compatible behavior for it.
This is REJECTED: the ARM manual says
software MUST NOT use those encodings.

Choice G: trust a disassembler's friendly
IT mnemonic even when the encoded
firstcond is disallowed. This is REJECTED
because LLVM 17 prints a valid-looking
name for BFF8 despite ARM's
UNPREDICTABLE restriction.

Choice H: model IT as one abstract
conditional higher-level operation
whose following instruction slots are
not separately represented.
This might simplify a source frontend
but would lose the bottom-up machine
instruction trace that motivates
the present English Major C experiment.
It remains a future elaboration
question, not a basis for erasing
the actual ITSTATE transitions.

The choice among legal B, IT and
branchless arithmetic constructions
cannot be determined by instruction
count alone. Actual target CPU and
input-distribution evidence remain
necessary for a performance assertion.

PART NINE. INDEPENDENT DIAGNOSTIC EVIDENCE AND LIMITS.

A foreign clang 17 diagnostic assembled
all 256 candidate halfwords as literal
.hword values for ARMv7-A T32. Each
candidate was followed by FOUR
NOP halfwords so that a correctly
decoded IT block could consume
its complete one-to-four-slot duration
before the next candidate was encountered.
The source did not claim that the
whole sequence represented a useful
application program or was executed.

LLVM objdump 17 decoded each candidate
with the expected exact 16-bit source
halfword value. It printed:
  one NOP;
  one YIELD;
  one WFE;
  one WFI;
  one SEV;
  eleven generic HINT mnemonics;
  240 IT-looking mnemonics.

The 240 mnemonic count includes the
26 UNPREDICTABLE encodings described
above. LLVM sometimes annotates an
unpredictable IT predicate sequence,
but in other cases prints an apparently
ordinary mnemonic, as with BFF8.
The ARM DDI rule supplies normative
legality, not LLVM's output alone.

This was a foreign assembler/disassembler
diagnostic on an x86-64 host, NOT a
maintained ICK/NDK compilation stage
or a running ARM processor.

PART TEN. ACCEPTANCE AND REJECTION OBLIGATIONS.

A future independent validator must:
  1. check exactly 256 unique halfwords
     BF00 through BFFF, with no omissions;
  2. distinguish mask zero from nonzero;
  3. recognize BF00 as the sole permitted
     NOP from this finite hint subgroup;
  4. recognize and exclude four named
     but ungranted hints BF10..BF40;
  5. reject all eleven reserved unallocated
     hints even though they execute as NOP;
  6. recognize the 214 conditionally
     admissible IT encodings without
     treating ITSTATE as ordinary user data;
  7. reject all fifteen firstcond-F
     nonzero-mask encodings;
  8. reject all eleven firstcond-E masks
     with bitcount other than one;
  9. reject any IT instruction encountered
     while already inside an active IT block;
 10. account for the actual two/four-byte
     width of each following IT slot;
 11. forbid T1 conditional branches inside
     IT regardless of their position;
 12. distinguish valid BFE8 from
     unpredictable BFF8 even if both
     are displayed "it al" by LLVM;
 13. preserve N/Z/C/V when IT itself
     executes; do not invent a data
     register write caused by IT alone;
 14. preserve the precise ITSTATE and
     appropriate condition handling
     across permitted following operations;
 15. reject an assertion that LLVM's
     mnemonic display proves hardware
     execution or source-level legality;
 16. refrain from claiming the complete
     Thumb ISA is accounted for merely
     because a BFxx subtree is closed.

The first seven and twelfth checks have
source-level fixture and decoder support.
The full ITSTATE transition and all
hostile instruction sequences are NOT
yet independently implemented or
qualified in an English Major C runtime.

END OF THE FINITE BF00..BFFF DECODE CONTRACT.
